#!/usr/bin/perl
# lzrprf: owns the one window of an application, runs the application inside a
# container that this file defines, and does for it the one thing it may have
# done: fetch the https address that you typed into the bar of that window.
#
#   lzrprf run /path/to/program [arguments]     open the window, with the program contained behind it
#   lzrprf incognito [/path/to/program [args]]  the same in an incognito window, which leaves nothing behind when it closes [C16]
#   lzrprf tor [/path/to/program [args]]        a Tor window: an incognito window of its own that Tor alone carries, sealed before it browses and ended in order [C17]
#   lzrprf run|incognito --profile ID /path/to/program [args]     the window of a profile other than the first, named by the id lzrprf made for it (12 hex digits), never by its name; it may stand before --open
#   lzrprf profiles                             print each profile's id and its name; --profile takes any id but "first", the profile used without it
#   lzrprf run|incognito --open "X Y W H" /path/to/program [args]   the window of a tab pulled out of another window, opened at that frame: the tab's address comes as one line on standard input, never as an argument, since every user of the computer can read the arguments of a process
#   lzrprf log [--profile ID]                   print what the log on this computer holds: the first profile's, or that profile's
#   lzrprf keep [--profile ID] [nothing|hosts|all [DAYS]]   say, or choose, what is kept after a window closes: of the first profile, or of that one
#   lzrprf address TEXT...                      say what would be fetched if TEXT were typed into the bar
#   lzrprf request [--other] TEXT...            print the exact bytes a site is sent for TEXT: as a page you visit, or with --other as a picture, a style sheet or the release file
#   lzrprf public NUMBER...                     say whether a numeric address is on the public internet, and how this computer would send to it
#   lzrprf filters DIR PAGE TYPE ADDRESS...     say what the filter lists in DIR (every NAME.txt, read unsigned) would do with each address as a request of TYPE from a page of the host PAGE [C27]
#   lzrprf lzr [command] [ID] [flags]           LZRTRMNL's command, lzr (its shell finds it on its PATH): with no command it lists them, and every one has --help [C20]
#   lzrprf mark                                 this build of macOS, this file and the selftest beside it (each file by its SHA-256), as one line; an empty line when no selftest can be read beside it
#
# The application is treated as hostile. It has no window, no network, no
# file of yours and no service of the system: it is started once for each answer
# lzrprf has already fetched, reads that answer, and writes a list of what to
# draw. lzrprf checks every line of the list and draws it. The window, its
# address bar, its buttons and its words are lzrprf's, made by the text at the
# end of this file.
#
# Five kinds of process are started here, each inside a container:
#   the PAGE container holds the application, once for each answer;
#   the FETCH container holds the system's own curl, once for each request;
#   the DECODER container holds the system's own sips, for a picture the application cannot decode;
#   the WINDOW container holds the window;
#   the SOUND container holds the sound process, which plays the sound of a page kept running
#   and the sound of a video whose sound you turned on, in step with its picture.
# What is outside every container is this file: it looks up the name, makes
# the one connection, writes the log, and checks what crosses.
#
# THE CONDITIONS. Each promise lzrprf makes is a condition in this file, and
# the line that holds it carries its mark. The README says the same in words
# and cites these marks, and selftest fails when a mark is cited that is not
# here, or the other way round.
#
#   [C1]  The application is started in one place only, and only inside the page container. It is handed a seal of its run, in LZRPRF_SEAL and again as the first line its keeper writes after the system said it is contained, and the application starts only with it.
#   [C2]  At every start lzrprf tries, from inside that same container, what the container must refuse, and starts nothing unless all of it was refused and the same tries worked outside.
#   [C3]  No process is handed a byte of what was fetched, nor the connection to a site, before the system itself says that this process is contained. Before that, of what you asked for each knows the address alone: the application in one variable, curl among its fixed switches. The one exception is the system's decoder [C21]: it reads its file from its start, so it is started by sandbox-exec, which starts nothing when its container cannot be made; the system is asked about it while it runs, and nothing it wrote is read unless the system said it was contained.
#   [C4]  A request is made only for an address that came from the window's bar or was typed into its own bookmark modal, at once or kept as the home page or a bookmark, or that a page redirected to and you then said yes to in the window, or for one of three fixed addresses of the release file after you said yes (the newest release number, a report, the installer in its one or two places), or for a picture the page laid out from such an address names, at most twenty-four, on that page's host, on a name under it (as img.example.com is for a page of example.com or www.example.com) or on at most eight other sites, each address checked as a typed one is and never inside your own network, or for a style sheet it names on that same host, at most eight, by a path lzrprf checked, or for a video it names, one file when you click its place in the window, held to the checks of its pictures [C22]. A support request you send from the cogwheel's menu, and the reading of its answer when you ask, go to the one support address the release file names and to no other place. There is no other way to a request, besides what a page kept running asks for within [C19]. Connect for a provider in a profile's Profiles list (Google, Apple or Meta) opens that provider's own fixed sign-in page, and a redirect from a host of a provider you connected or are connecting to another host of that same family is followed without a question, written down first, at most 8 a visit; a redirect out of the family is asked about as every other.
#   [C5]  Nothing is looked up or fetched before its line is in the log: in the memory of the process behind the window, and on the disk too when you chose to keep a log. No answer is shown whose line could not be recorded.
#   [C6]  There is one place that connects to a site directly, and it connects to a numeric address that was checked and written down; the one other connection to the outside goes to the Tor client on this computer's loopback, only while Tor is on [C17].
#   [C7]  What a site is sent is made from the address alone, with one fixed browser name, LZRBRWSR, and, only on the request of a page you visit, one fixed line of the kinds of answer it takes, Accept: text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8: the same request from every computer, byte for byte, with nothing kept from an earlier request but the cookies below. A page kept running adds to it only what [C19] lets it. A support request adds only its body: your words with the release number, the picture of the window when you ticked it, and your licence code when one is kept; reading its answer adds only the key lzrprf was handed for it. The one exception is a provider you connected in a profile's window: a request to a host of that provider's own family (google.com, youtube.com and gmail.com for Google; apple.com and icloud.com for Apple; facebook.com, messenger.com and instagram.com for Meta), as the request of a page you visit or of a page kept running of that same family, carries the cookies that family set since you pressed Connect, matched by host and path, on curl's input and never on a command line; no request of another site's page carries them, no value is logged, and an incognito window or Tor carries none. A second exception is a compatibility identity, the closed part's decision (SYSTEM.md D19): where the application says a request is to be sent as a mainstream browser, by the word safari on a page's request or for the hosts it names to lzrprf once a window, that request carries Safari's User-Agent, Accept, Accept-Language and Sec-Fetch-* lines in Safari's order in place of the fixed ones; lzrprf holds the one fixed set of lines for that word and no list of sites of its own, a word it holds no set for ends the page, and through Tor none is ever sent. A third exception is the cookie jar (the owner's decision of 2026-11-24: same domain keeps its cookies across pages): the request of a page you visit, and of a page kept running outside the sandbox, carries the line Cookie that the application's jar gives for that address under the page it is for, on curl's input and never on a command line, and each Set-Cookie line of its answer is handed to the jar. The jar is a run of the application in the page container (sub jar_started), never lzrprf and never a page; it keeps a site's cookies apart under each top-level site, so no site sees what it set under another. A page is handed, in front of its answer, only the cookies of its own host its script may see, checked by lzrprf (sub jar_frame), and its script's writes are judged by the jar against the page's address as lzrprf knows it. For a host of a provider you connected or are connecting, that provider's store is used and the jar is not asked; no value is logged.
#   [C8]  A host inside your own network is fetched only after you said yes to exactly that, once, and never for a picture or a video.
#   [C9]  Every line the application writes is checked before the window is given it, and what is not one of five kinds of line, or a laid page, is not drawn at all. Its title, one line of at most 200 characters with every control and format character taken out, is shown in its tab and nowhere else.
#   [C10] The window's process has no network of its own, writes no file, starts no other program, opens nothing through the system, commands no other program, sends no signal to another process, and reads nothing under /Users or on another disk but your settings, fonts, keyboard layouts and input methods. Before the window takes an address or shows a fetched page, lzrprf asks the system about eight operations of that process (connecting out, creating a file, starting a second process, reaching a system service when none is named, opening something through the system, commanding another program, writing your settings, sending a signal), and each must be refused; the rest of that container's text, its list of services among it, is not asked about, and selftest makes its own tries from the real window. Of the system's services it looks up by name only the 42 its container lists, the 41 a window was seen to ask for and the system's video decoder [C22], and one local name, the clipboard's. It reads one kind of a site's bytes itself: a video you clicked, whose MP4 container the system's player reads and whose sound it decodes in this process, with only the pictures decoded by the system's service [C22].
#   [C11] No page is fetched or shown before selftest held on this build of macOS with this file and the selftest beside it, in its last run there. The window is there earlier, with its bar switched off: it shows lzrprf's own questions and no page. A check the test could not try there is named in its mark, and then no page is fetched or shown before you said yes to starting without exactly those checks.
#   [C12] Nothing of what you fetched stays on the disk unless you chose a kept log, apart from the one address you made the home page, the bookmarks you made or imported (each a name and an address) and the order of each of their folders (a file .order of their names), whether the bookmarks bar is shown, whether pictures from other sites are blocked, whether LZRDFNDR's satellite plays in the home page's minigame (gamehelper), and an installer you chose to run, with what it printed (install.txt), a licence code you entered (licence), the reference and key of the support request you sent last (support), never its words, and the script permissions you chose to keep (scripts: each a site, where the script came from, its SHA-256, its capabilities and the day; scriptlayers: whether the sandbox is off and which curated rules you switched off) [C26], and the filter lists you switched off and the hosts you let through on a site (filterlayers) [C27], and the notes you wrote to programs in the tool's Show all processes (procnotes) [C20]; a kept log holds hosts only unless you chose full addresses. It is only added to, apart from clearing by your button and the days you chose: in a kept log each of the two says so in its first line, and with nothing kept, clearing removes the log whole and the window's lines say so. The lasting cookies of the sites a window of a profile visited are kept in that profile's file cookies, sealed by the application with the key of 32 random bytes in cookies.key, both readable by you alone, until they expire or clearing the sessions or Clear Cookies of This Site clears them. The cookies of a provider you connected are kept in that profile's file sso/PROVIDER, readable by you alone, from the moment its session cookie appears (looked for by its name, never its value) until you press Remove; no clearing of history, sessions or the log touches them.
#   [C13] The application opens no file for its fonts: lzrprf hands it eight fonts of the Mac, by fixed paths, in front of a page's answer and never a picture's, each a plain file and no link of at most 2 MiB that begins as a font, or with length 0; and only after the system said that the application is contained.
#   [C14] A page is laid out at most three times: a second time only when its first run named style sheets on the page's own host, at most eight, or named no sheet and web fonts its own <style> names [C30], which lzrprf fetches and hands the second run in front of the answer, after the fonts, each at most 1 MiB or with length 0. A second run that is shown and names web fonts [C30] is followed by a third, handed the same sheets and those fonts, shown when it is a page that names neither again, else the second run's. A second run that names style sheets again is not shown, the first run's page is. A second run whose program or container failed shows that fault, as a first run's would, and no page. A second run that is no page (unfit, ended or late) and asked for no address while it was read is followed by a third, handed each named sheet with length 0, which the application reads with the network it withholds from a first run that names style sheets, so the page's external and module scripts run; its page is shown when it is a page and names no style sheet, else the first run's. A second run that asked for an address, or wrote too much, has no third: the first run's page is shown, and the bar says that none of its external or module scripts ran in it. No run of a visit asks again for what an earlier run of it asked. A run laid again, for a new width or a tab chosen again, asks again. A page kept running asks as [C19] allows. A first run that named only web fonts is read without the network, as one that names sheets; the second is handed those fonts and is shown when it is a page that names neither again; when it is no page (unfit, ended or late) and asked for no address, a third is handed each font with length 0 and read with the network, shown when it is such a page; else the first run's page is. There is never a fourth run, and a picture run is handed no style sheet.
#   [C16] An incognito window writes nothing that outlives it. Its log, history, tabs, suggestions and everything it fetched are held in its memory or in one folder it makes for itself, readable by you alone, in your own temporary folder (TMPDIR, /private/var/folders/.../T), named lzrprf-i.<its process number>.<12 random hex digits>. Of ~/.lzrprf it reads, and never writes, the self-test's result (proven, untested, selftest.lock), the home page, the search address, whether the bookmarks bar is shown, whether pictures from other sites are blocked, whether LZRDFNDR's satellite plays in the minigame, the bookmarks, the script permissions (scripts, scriptlayers, and the curated rules with the signed manifest they were installed from) and the filter lists with that manifest and your switches of them (filterlayers), and holds in its memory every script permission and switch chosen in it; opened from the window of a profile other than the first, it reads these five of that profile's folder instead, by the id on its command line. The folder is removed when the window ends, however it ends: quit, its last window closed, a signal, its process dying; and a folder left by a run whose processes are all gone is removed at the next start of any lzrprf window. It reads and writes no provider's sign-in; the cookies its sites set are held by a jar of its own in memory alone, never sealed or written, and end with it.
#   [C17] Tor carries a Tor window alone, never a window that is not one: the onion of such a window opens a Tor window apart from it, a fresh incognito session of its own [C16] that shares no cookie, cache, storage or kept answer with it, either way. In a Tor window lzrprf starts the client that stands beside the program, named by its SHA-256 in the release file, in a container of its own (its own folder and the network, nothing of yours); the window is sealed (no name is looked up on this computer and no connection goes past Tor) before it browses, and every address is then fetched through Tor, the site's host its SOCKS user so each site has its own circuit; nothing is looked up or fetched directly, also while it connects, when it does not, or after Tor went down. Stop Tor, in the Tor window's own modal while it connects or once it is ready, asks first (all its tabs are closed, Tor is disconnected and the window closed) and closes the whole window, in this order: no new navigation, its pages and their processes ended, its data removed from its folder and its tables in memory, that checked, and only then the client is ended. With Tor on, no cookie of a connected provider is read, kept or sent; each Tor identity has a jar of its own, in memory alone, ended with it.
#   [C15] Another browser's bookmarks are read by lzrprf's reader alone, a system perl in a container that lets it read under your home folder these files and nothing else: Local State and each profile's Bookmarks of Chrome, Edge, Brave and Vivaldi, Firefox's profiles.ini and bookmarkbackups/*.jsonlz4, Safari's Bookmarks.plist; it has no network, writes nothing and starts no program but itself. It looks whether each browser's folder is there when the import wizard opens, and reads a browser's files only after you chose that browser; each a plain file of yours of one name, no link and in no linked folder, of at most 32 MiB; inside a browser's folder it may see no other file. When macOS keeps the reader out of a browser other than Safari, the window's Open panel asks for that browser's own folder, once a browser in an import, and only Allow on that folder has the reader try again; whether macOS then lets it through only a real Mac shows. An HTML bookmarks file you chose is read by lzrprf itself. Nothing in them is fetched, and nothing of them is written but the bookmarks imported [C12].
#   [C18] A page kept running with its tab is handed four kinds of line and nothing else: a click, a key, text typed while it says a field of it has the focus, and where the window shows it from (its scroll), each checked in the window and here; what it writes back is checked as a first list is, an address it asks for is a visit like a typed one, and it ends with its tab, at most three at once.
#   [C19] A page kept running with its tab, from the moment it is read, may ask lzrprf for an address with GET, HEAD or POST, one at a time and at most 600 times in its life: each is checked as an address typed into the bar is, written down first and fetched through the one place that connects, carrying the fixed request of [C7] with nothing added but, for a POST, at most 64 KiB of the page's body and one of three types, and for another host the page's own Origin; the page is handed back the status, six headers and the body, and nothing else. A page lzrprf's sandbox runs has the other scripts its answer names by a script element fetched ahead once it asks for its first external script, at most 64, at most 12 at a time, each judged, filtered, looked up and written down first as the page's own request would be, with that very request: an answer is handed over only when the page then asks for exactly that request, and one it does not ask for is dropped unread with the visit; while the page is read, an answer it was handed is handed again for the very same request of its next layout in that visit (sub prefetch_begin, sub prefetch_go, sub prefetch_keep).
#   [C21] A picture the application cannot decode, an AVIF, HEIC or HEIF file it hands back whole, is decoded by the system's own sips in the decoder container: a folder of its own that lzrprf makes for each picture and removes after, where it reads one file, the picture, and writes one, a PNG of at most 1024 pixels a side, and a folder within for its own temporary file; no network, no other file of yours, at most 30 seconds of processor time and 10 seconds in all for each of its runs, at most two. It may look up one service of the system by name, its video decoder (com.apple.coremedia.videodecoder), and open one kind of connection to a driver, the graphics surfaces (IOSurfaceRootUserClient), which sips opens itself and without which it decodes nothing (tried on 2026-11-21): the decoding itself is done in that service, by the operating system, outside every container of lzrprf and beyond its limits, in Apple's own sandbox for that service. sips starts only once the system said its process is contained: a perl waits for lzrprf's word and then becomes sips, the one other program that container lets start. Every byte sips is given is the site's: lzrprf hands it the body curl fetched, and only when the application handed back that same file. The PNG goes to the application as a picture's answer, so what the window is given is still pixels lzrprf checked [C9].
#   [C20] The application's tool, opened by its icon in the bottom bar and never in an incognito window: lzrprf reads this computer's processes and launchd jobs with the system's own programs (ps, launchctl, plutil, codesign) and hands what it saw to the application in its container, which has no network and no file, so nothing of it leaves this computer. A look changes nothing. lzrprf changes something only for a row you ticked and then confirmed, where each change is said in lzrprf's own words and written in the log first: a launchd job of your account is switched off (launchctl disable, then bootout), a process of yours is asked to end (TERM); never a job or a process of the system or of Apple, never lzrprf's own, never with a password. The jobs you switched off are kept in ~/.lzrprf/quieted, looked at again at each start and every five minutes while a window is open, switched off again when something switched them on, and switched on again when you say so. A button above the list copies the title and the description of each row you ticked to the clipboard, under one sentence saying where they were found, so you can paste them where you like; it takes a press only while a row is ticked. Show all processes, at the bottom left of the list, adds every process of the look with its share of a processor and its memory (ps), what it is from the application's word or else the manual page of its program on this Mac, and the note you wrote to its program, kept in ~/.lzrprf/procnotes; nothing of it is sent anywhere, and a process is changed only as a ticked row of the list above. Each process's Copy info copies what lzrprf gathered of it to the clipboard, and nothing else of the tool is written to the clipboard. LZRTRMNL's command lzr ("lzrprf lzr") reads the same processes with ps, and of a process of yours what macOS tells you of it (its footprint, its arguments); it never asks the system for more than any user may see. It changes something only on your word, a question on the terminal or --yes, said in lzrprf's own words, written in the log first and held to the same rules: it ends a process of yours (TERM, then KILL three seconds on); it deactivates a program, kept in ~/.lzrprf/deactivated, its launchd jobs of your account switched off, each of its processes ended whenever lzrprf sees one, at every lzr command and every ten seconds while a window is open; or it contains a process of yours, ended and its program started again with the same arguments in the page container, with no variable, no byte written into a file and no memory dump, as the child of a keeper of its own that ends it at once when the system does not say it is contained; never a program of the system or of LZRBRWSR, never lzrprf's own.
#   [C22] A video is fetched only when you click its place in the window: an MP4 file or an HLS stream the page names on its =videos line, held to the checks of a picture on another site [C4] [C5] [C8], fetched once and whole by the fixed request of a picture [C7], at most 64 MiB in 120 seconds, and handed to the window in base64. The window holds it in its memory, at most two videos at once, and gives it to the system's player (AVFoundation) through a loader of its own, at most 4 MiB at a time, so the player reaches no network and reads no file. That player runs in the window's own process: it reads the MP4 container and decodes the sound (AAC) there, in the window's container, and hands only the pictures' bitstream to the system's video decoder service, the one name the window's container lists for it, which decodes them outside every container of lzrprf, in Apple's own sandbox for that service. So, as for no picture, system code in the window's process reads bytes a site chose: a flaw in that code that a site's file reaches hands the site the window's process, and with it what lzrprf trusts the window to say (what you type in its bar, your answers to its questions). A click on a video that loads stops it. Nothing of a video reaches the application. An HLS stream is followed by lzrprf itself: its playlist, at most 256 KiB, every address in it https on the playlist's own site, all checked before any is fetched, one quality chosen up front, at most 1024 segments, each in the log first, within the same cap and clock, joined into one MPEG-TS or fragmented MP4 file the window plays as an MP4 (an MPEG-TS stream with sound made into an MP4 first [C24]); a stream locked by a key or by DRM is refused; an address of a playlist that is refused is said by its scheme and host alone [C12].
#   [C23] No address on the list of child sexual abuse material and other illegal content is looked up, fetched, shown, kept or written. The list is the file blocklist beside lzrprf, digests only (the MD5 of an onion address, the SHA-256 of a host and of an address), read once at the start of a window and matched on this computer alone: nothing of what you visit is sent anywhere to be matched. Every address is held to it before anything else is done with it: what is typed into the bar or searched for, a bookmark, the home page, every hop of a redirect, a picture, a style sheet, a video, what a page kept running asks for, the fixed addresses of the release file, with Tor on as with Tor off, and once more where a name is looked up, where curl is started and where Tor is asked to connect. An onion address is matched by its last two labels, so every name under it is the same, and any other host by itself and by every name it stands under. An address is put in one form before it is matched (sub url_form, form 2, named in the list's first line), so another spelling of it is the same address, and is matched with every choice of its query's parts, without its query and at every path above its own down to its first segment; one too long to be read is refused. A listed address is refused with one sentence in the window and the same sentence in the log, which names no address; a line that would name one is written as that sentence instead. No setting, variable or button turns this off: a list that is missing, damaged, or another file than the application's release file names stops every page, and the window says why.
#   [C24] An MPEG-TS stream with sound that lzrprf joined for a video you clicked [C22] is made into an MP4 by the system's own /usr/bin/avconvert, its pictures and sound copied as they came and not decoded (PresetPassthrough), in the converter container: a folder of its own that lzrprf makes for each stream and removes after, in incognito too, where it reads one file, the stream, and writes one, the MP4, and a folder within for its own copy; no network, no other file of yours, at most 30 seconds of processor time and 30 seconds in all, files of at most 96 MiB. It may look up two services of the system by name, the register of sound decoders (com.apple.audio.AudioComponentRegistrar) and the read-only map of file types (com.apple.lsd.mapdb), without which it reads no sound and writes no MP4 (tried 2026-11-21); the window's container lists neither, so the window plays the MP4 as any MP4. avconvert starts only once the system said its process is contained: a perl waits for lzrprf's word and then becomes avconvert, the one other program that container lets start. Every byte it is given is the site's, joined by lzrprf; the MP4 is taken only when it is a plain file with one name, at most 64 MiB, whose first box is ftyp.
#   [C25] An update is offered, and its installer run, only for a release whose manifest is signed with the release key written here ($RELEASE_KEY): checked by macOS's own ssh-keygen in the verifier container, of the published number, newer than yours, not expired, and naming the installer by the SHA-256 it must have; there is no way around a refusal.
#   [C26] No script runs that you have not allowed. lzrprf holds the only policy and the only store (scripts in the state folder, one rule a line, each of a class: a script's code, or its turning text into code, asked on its own; read strictly: a file that does not read means you are asked, never that anything runs) and tells each page run, after its style sheets, which scripts of its site run and which do not (=permits); a page asks lzrprf for any other one and is answered at once, deny for what no rule allows, which is put to you once the page is drawn, each script by its name and with its source shown as text that never runs: Deny Once, the answer when nobody answers and kept for this visit only, Deny Always, a deny rule kept so it is not asked again, Allow Once or Always Allow, after which the page is loaded again. Four layers decide, in this order, and a Deny of yours wins over each: the sandbox (E1), the curated rules (E2), your rules and Allow Once (E3), and then you are asked. In the sandbox, which one switch of LZRDFNDR scripts turns off, a site with no rule of yours runs its own scripts (inline, or from its own origin, asking for capabilities of low risk) with no question, and lzrprf refuses (403, never fetched or sent) every POST of the page and every other request of it to another origin but a script or module, which is asked about as before; it sends no cookie and writes nothing for the site. A curated rule is a reviewed line of the file permit-rules the installer kept beside the release's signed manifest, used only while that file's SHA-256 is the one the signed manifest names, never one that grants a sensitive action [C5], a whole domain or a page of signing in; each can be switched off. A page with a password field, or at an address of signing in, has no sandbox and no curated rule. A site whose scripts are not allowed has every script element, handler attribute, javascript: address and srcdoc taken out of its page and its frames, and none of its script files fetched; script bytes a rule denies are fetched, hashed and not handed over. A POST to another host than the page's is never covered by a script permission: you are asked for it, and while a page is read it is refused.
#   [C27] No request a filter list blocks is looked up or fetched, and no filter list decides whether a script runs. The lists are uBlock Origin's uAssets lists, kept as their makers wrote them under their own licence, and lzrprf's own reviewed exceptions, read as data from beside lzrprf only while the release's signed manifest names each on its own line by its SHA-256 and its approval and signature hold, as for the curated rules of [C26]; anything else, and nothing is filtered, and Filters in the cogwheel's menu says why. lzrprf holds no code of uBlock Origin: it reads the published filter syntax, network rules and their exceptions of the kinds docs/filters.md lists, and counts every other line, cosmetic rules, scriptlets and code among them, without running or guessing at it. Every picture, style sheet and video lzrprf fetches for a page and every request of a page kept running is held to the lists after [C23] and before [C26]; an address you typed, a bookmark and the home page are not. A blocked request is refused, and the log says which rule of which list blocked it. An exception only means not blocked: a script it lets through is still decided by [C26], and a line that looks like a permission is refused when its list is read. You may switch a list off and let one host through on one site, each kept in filterlayers, shown in Filters and revoked there.
#   [C28] Before a page, picture, sheet, video, stream part or script request is looked up or fetched, the application judges its address in a separate sealed page-container run. Only a complete, well-formed judgement from a successfully ended run can allow it; a blocked, missing, malformed, unfit or failed judgement sends nothing to that site. At most 64 addresses are judged in one run and at most 2048 verdicts are cached, in memory for this window and UTC day alone. A page the application's content shield, LZRDFNDR, blocks is not drawn and none of it runs: the application writes one line, =flagged, its confidence, list, category and host and path, checked here to its form, and the window shows a red page with two buttons, Close the Tab (the first, and the answer when the time is over) and Report False Positive Anonymously; none shows the page. lzrprf holds none of the shield's lists. A report is shown whole before it is sent, holds the category, the list, the confidence and the host and path and nothing else, is sent only on Send to the release file's flagreport address, through Tor when Tor is on and not at all when Tor is on and not connected, and the tab closes either way.
#   [C29] A terminal (LZRTRMNL) starts only on a press of the local home's terminal icon, and never in an incognito or Tor window. lzrprf opens the system's pseudo-terminal and starts your login shell, one /etc/shells lists, as you, with no descriptor of lzrprf's; the screen is drawn by core-lzrbrwsr-trmnl in a container of its own that may read only its program, the system's library cache and the root folder, and holds no descriptor of the terminal. Only your keys are written to the shell, each named key in one fixed form, and of the container's words only the answers a program asked its terminal for, once per question, in their one form. Every line it draws is checked as a laid page's line is. What you paste is written to the shell as text with no control character but tabs and line ends, bracketed when the shell asked for it; the window reads the clipboard only on your paste, and writes it only with the text you selected, on your copy, checked as text. A title a program sets is the tab's title as plain text of at most 80 characters, and nothing a program writes reaches the clipboard. A pane split from another, or a terminal tab added, starts another such shell only on your press, an item of the terminal's menus or one of its own keys; Save tabs and views writes only the tabs, the splits and their sizes and each shell's working folder to the file trmnl of this profile's folder, readable by you alone, and a pane opened from it is a new login shell in that folder. A press of yours is written to the shell, as the one report of a press and its release, only while the program asked for presses, seen by lzrprf itself in what it wrote, and only for a click you made in the window, at the cell the container named within the screen. The terminal opens with a picture of its name drawn by the container, and its last lines say how to start with lzr.
#   [C30] A web font is fetched only when a run handed the page's style sheets names it on its =webfonts line, or the first run of a page that names no sheet names it from its own <style>: at most 8 fonts, each by up to 4 paths on the page's own host tried in order until one answers 200 (a src entry that fails falls through to the next), each path held to the checks of a sheet ([C23], then [C27] as a font, [C28], [C5] its line in the log first, [C6] [C7] through the page's own checked addresses and through Tor when Tor is on), each at most 2 MiB and 64 KiB, at most 8 MiB together, all begun within 10 seconds; anything else goes with length 0. The answers are handed to the next run in front of the answer, after the style sheets (the third, or the second of a page that names no sheet), and kept in memory for the page shown alone: nothing of a font is written to the disk, in an incognito window or any. The application unpacks and checks each in its container and draws text in its own faces wherever a font is refused.
#   [C31] The live debug link sends only while you switched it on in the cogwheel's menu of a window that is neither incognito nor Tor, for at most 24 hours from that press, and the window shows its badge with its id the whole time. It sends to one fixed address, https://staging.lzrbrwsr.com/link/ followed by its id, as a fixed request is sent (sub fixed: written down first, curl in its container): the times of each load, request, lookup and application run, each address as its scheme, host and path alone, status codes, sizes and errors in lzrprf's own words; never a cookie, what you type or paste, a form's contents, a page's text, a query or a fragment. Its answer may hold commands; lzrprf runs one only when it is signed for the namespace lzrbrwsr-debug-link by an approval key lzrprf carries, names this link's id, carries a nonce never seen before and an expiry within 15 minutes, and is one of six: open an address in a new tab (an address so opened is checked as a typed one is [C4]), reload the tab shown, measure a load, a snapshot of the tabs and timings, the verbosity of the lines, a picture of the window from its own drawing. Anything else is refused and written down; no command runs a program, reads a file or reaches a shell.
#
# This file is the whole program. Its parts, in order: the containers, the
# proof that they hold, one visit, the fetch, the window's side, the log, the
# window itself.
